-- 1175: Blog post - "Germination, Step by Step: Our 8 October 2016 Hydroponics Class"
--
--       Eighth post for WSQ - Basic Urban Farming with Hydroponics (TGS-2025053916).
--       The 8 Oct 2016 class at Toh Orchid Farm, taught by Geoffrey Chow (same
--       trainer as 1174's 25 Jul 2015 class). Cross-links to five sibling posts.
--       Ships already published (status 1); linkedin_urn carries the manual-skip
--       sentinel (back-dated post - standing rule, see the blog-pipeline skill).
--       published_at pinned to 2016-10-08.
--
-- ANGLE: deliberately NOT a repeat of 1174. Both are Geoffrey Chow classes at the
-- same venue, so this one is built around what its OWN photographs show - the
-- projected germination-stages photo, and the two different lids (4-hole vs 6-hole)
-- visible on the tables, which carry a real spacing lesson. 1174 is about the
-- practitioner-taught cascade NFT slide and the kit-per-learner rule. Check both
-- before editing either, so they do not converge into near-duplicates.
--
-- TRAINER NAME: the admin wrote "Greogrey Chow" here and "Geogrey Chow" for 1174;
-- the 2015 slide credit read "GChow". The copy uses the conventional
-- "Geoffrey Chow" in both - flag to the admin if his preferred spelling differs.
-- Claims are limited to what the photos support: he taught this class, from that
-- germination slide. No biography or credentials asserted.
--
-- Every course figure was read off the LIVE course page on 2026-08-29: $350 before
-- GST, GST $31.50 (9%), nett $206.50 / $136.50, 1 day / 8 hrs at MEOD Farm, funding
-- valid 24 Mar 2025 - 23 Mar 2027, TSC AGR-FOP-2002-1.1.
--
-- VENUE: 2016 class at Toh Orchid Farm (past tense); MEOD Farm named as the CURRENT
-- venue. Today's venue is not retro-fitted onto the 2016 photos.
--
-- CERTIFICATE: the photo shows a certificate handshake but the document is not
-- legible, so the copy says only "a certificate ... from the course provider" and
-- contrasts it with today's WSQ Certificate of Achievement + SkillsFuture OpenCert.
-- No specific 2016 certificate title is asserted (unlike 1174, where the wording
-- WAS legible in the photograph).
--
-- Photographs: 7 selected from a 12-shot album. 1008px-native 2016 exports averaging
-- 103KB, already under the <150KB target, so uploaded AS-IS - re-encoding would only
-- destroy quality for no saving. Total 727KB, all verified HTTP 200.
-- Under blog/hydroponics/2016-10-08-*.
--
-- Notes for future editors:
--  * apply.php splits statements on ";" at end-of-line - the content HTML is ONE line.
--  * Content is pure ASCII; excerpt uses a REAL em-dash via CHAR() (see 1161);
--    meta_description carries NO em-dash entity (see 1164).
--  * FAQ ships as native <details> accordions per the blog-pipeline rules.
--  * Idempotent: WHERE NOT EXISTS on url_key, INSERT IGNORE on tag links.
--  * SG-only guard via @mms_instance.
--  * hero_image_url is NOT set here - generated on prod post-deploy (farming theme).

SET @is_sg := IF(@mms_instance = 'SG', 1, 0);


INSERT INTO `mmd_blog_post` (`title`, `url_key`, `excerpt`, `content`, `author`, `status`, `published_at`, `related_skus`, `source_sku`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `meta_keywords`, `linkedin_urn`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) SELECT 'Germination, Step by Step: Our 8 October 2016 Hydroponics Class', 'hydroponics-class-2016-germination-toh-orchid-farm', CONCAT('Most hydroponic failures happen in the first fortnight, before the system is even interesting ', CHAR(0xE2,0x80,0x94 USING utf8mb4), ' at germination. Our 8 October 2016 class at Toh Orchid Farm, taught by Geoffrey Chow, spent the day on the part beginners usually rush.'), '<p>Ask why a first hydroponic attempt failed and the answer is rarely about pumps or nutrient recipes. It is almost always that the seeds never got going properly &mdash; patchy germination, seedlings that stalled at two leaves, a tray where half the sites came up and half did not.</p> <p>Our class on <strong>8 October 2016</strong> at <strong>Toh Orchid Farm</strong>, taught by <strong>Geoffrey Chow</strong>, gave that first fortnight the attention it deserves.</p> <figure class="mmd-blog-figure"> <img src="https://pub-77c0dec029944b0386e40673ce81081f.r2.dev/blog/hydroponics/2016-10-08-class-group-photo.jpg" alt="The large 8 October 2016 hydroponics class posing together at the front of the training room at Toh Orchid Farm" /> <figcaption>The 8 October 2016 class at Toh Orchid Farm. Photo: Tertiary Infotech Academy.</figcaption> </figure> <h2>The lesson on the screen</h2> <figure class="mmd-blog-figure"> <img src="https://pub-77c0dec029944b0386e40673ce81081f.r2.dev/blog/hydroponics/2016-10-08-geoffrey-chow-teaching.jpg" alt="Geoffrey Chow presenting to the class beside a projected photograph of a row of pots with seedlings at different stages of germination" /> <figcaption>Geoffrey Chow teaching from a photograph of seedlings at successive stages &mdash; what normal progress actually looks like. Photo: Tertiary Infotech Academy.</figcaption> </figure> <p>Look at what is projected: a row of pots showing seedlings at successive stages. Not a diagram &mdash; a photograph of real trays, so the class could see what healthy progress looks like day by day.</p> <p>That matters more than it sounds. The commonest beginner mistake is not a technical error at all; it is <strong>impatience</strong>. Someone sees nothing on day three, decides the nutrients are wrong, doubles the strength, and burns the seedlings that were about to emerge. Knowing what day three is supposed to look like prevents an entire category of self-inflicted failure.</p> <h2>Everyone gets a system</h2> <figure class="mmd-blog-figure"> <img src="https://pub-77c0dec029944b0386e40673ce81081f.r2.dev/blog/hydroponics/2016-10-08-kits-on-the-tables.jpg" alt="Hydroponic grow tubs with white lids on classroom tables, one with four planting holes and another with six, alongside notes and supplies" /> <figcaption>Kits on the tables. Note the different lids &mdash; four planting sites on one, six on another. Photo: Tertiary Infotech Academy.</figcaption> </figure> <p>Look closely at the two lids: one has four planting holes, the other six. That is not an accident of supply, it is a teaching point about <strong>spacing</strong>.</p> <p>Six sites in the same tub means more plants and less room for each. For fast, small leafy greens that is fine. For anything that fills out, the four-hole lid gives each plant the air circulation it needs, and crowded leaves that trap humidity between them are an invitation to disease. Choosing the lid is choosing the crop &mdash; before a single seed is sown.</p> <figure class="mmd-blog-figure"> <img src="https://pub-77c0dec029944b0386e40673ce81081f.r2.dev/blog/hydroponics/2016-10-08-practice-session.jpg" alt="Participants seated at tables working with their black hydroponic grow tubs and lids during the practical session" /> <figcaption>The practical session: every participant working on their own system. Photo: Tertiary Infotech Academy.</figcaption> </figure> <figure class="mmd-blog-figure"> <img src="https://pub-77c0dec029944b0386e40673ce81081f.r2.dev/blog/hydroponics/2016-10-08-classroom-session.jpg" alt="Wide view of the training room mid-session, with participants at tables and a projected slide at the front" /> <figcaption>The room mid-session, kits out on every table. Photo: Tertiary Infotech Academy.</figcaption> </figure> <p>The structure has never changed: a short block of theory, then straight to the tubs. The lecture exists to make the practical make sense, not the other way round.</p> <h2>The questions a full room produces</h2> <figure class="mmd-blog-figure"> <img src="https://pub-77c0dec029944b0386e40673ce81081f.r2.dev/blog/hydroponics/2016-10-08-class-in-session.jpg" alt="A full classroom of participants seated at tables listening during the hydroponics session, kits and notes in front of them" /> <figcaption>A full room. The questions people bring are half the value of teaching in person. Photo: Tertiary Infotech Academy.</figcaption> </figure> <p>A room this size always surfaces the same handful of questions, and they are worth answering here because they are almost certainly yours too:</p> <ul> <li><strong>"Nothing has come up. Is it dead?"</strong> Usually not, and usually it is too early. Different seeds have genuinely different timelines, and temperature shifts them. Resist adjusting anything before you have given it the days it needs.</li> <li><strong>"Should I add more nutrient to speed it up?"</strong> No. A germinating seed lives on its own reserves and needs very little. Too strong a solution at that stage does damage rather than good.</li> <li><strong>"Why did only some sites come up?"</strong> Almost always uneven moisture in the medium &mdash; a sponge dry in the middle, or one sitting slightly higher and not wicking. It is a mechanical problem, not a nutrient one.</li> <li><strong>"How much light do seedlings need?"</strong> More than most people give them, and early. A seedling stretching tall and thin is reaching for light it cannot find, and it will not recover from that leggy start.</li> </ul> <h2>Ending the day with something that works</h2> <figure class="mmd-blog-figure"> <img src="https://pub-77c0dec029944b0386e40673ce81081f.r2.dev/blog/hydroponics/2016-10-08-certificate-presentation.jpg" alt="A participant shaking hands with the trainer while receiving his course certificate, with classmates smiling around them" /> <figcaption>Certificates at the end of the day. Photo: Tertiary Infotech Academy.</figcaption> </figure> <p>Everyone left with a certificate and, more usefully, an assembled system and a realistic idea of what the first two weeks would look like. In 2016 that certificate came from the course provider. Today the course carries WSQ accreditation, a formal assessment and an OpenCert from SkillsFuture Singapore &mdash; but the thing that determines whether you succeed is still whether you know what day three should look like.</p> <h2>The course today</h2> <p><a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-basic-urban-farming-with-hydroponics.html">WSQ - Basic Urban Farming with Hydroponics</a> (course code TGS-2025053916) is a <strong>1-day, 8-hour</strong> class at Beginner level with a 1-hour assessment, held at <strong>MEOD Farm, 13 Neo Tiew Harvest Lane</strong>. It maps to the SkillsFuture Skills Framework TSC <strong>Urban Farming Implementation and Management Control (AGR-FOP-2002-1.1)</strong>, with two learning outcomes:</p> <ol> <li>Perform farming activities and equipment operations according to standard operating procedures</li> <li>Execute farming operations work in accordance with regulations, WSH practices and housekeeping rules</li> </ol> <p>The day covers hydroponic system types (deep water culture, nutrient film technique, wick, ebb and flow, drip), growing media, plant nutrition and nutrient solutions, pH and EC monitoring, indoor farming conditions, Singapore''s urban farming regulations, good agriculture practices and workplace safety &mdash; plus hands-on practice with a hydroponics kit and a guided tour of a commercial hydroponics farm.</p> <h3>What it costs</h3> <p>The full fee is <strong>$350</strong> before GST. With WSQ funding:</p> <table> <tr><th>Who you are</th><th>Nett fee</th><th>Funding</th></tr> <tr><td>Singapore Citizen / PR, aged 21 and above</td><td><strong>$206.50</strong></td><td>50% course-fee funding</td></tr> <tr><td>Singapore Citizen aged 40 and above (MCES), or SME-sponsored</td><td><strong>$136.50</strong></td><td>70% course-fee funding</td></tr> </table> <p>GST of $31.50 (9%) applies to the full course fee. Funding is valid <strong>from 24 March 2025 to 23 March 2027</strong>. Eligible Singapore Citizens can apply <strong>SkillsFuture Credit</strong> and <strong>PSEA</strong> against the nett fee, NTUC members may claim <strong>UTAP</strong>, and employers can tap <strong>SFEC</strong> with <strong>Absentee Payroll</strong> support. Completion &mdash; at least 75% attendance plus a pass in the assessment &mdash; earns a Certificate of Achievement from Tertiary Infotech Academy and an <strong>OpenCert (Statement of Achievement) from SkillsFuture Singapore</strong>.</p> <p><a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-basic-urban-farming-with-hydroponics.html"><strong>Check the next class date and funding eligibility &rarr;</strong></a></p> <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2> <div class="mmd-faq"> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">How long should germination take?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>It depends on the seed and the temperature, and the honest answer is that it varies more than beginners expect. The useful discipline is to know roughly what your chosen crop should do and leave it alone until then. Most first-attempt failures come from intervening too early rather than from anything being genuinely wrong.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Should I use full-strength nutrient on seedlings?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>No. A germinating seed runs on its own stored reserves and needs very little, so a strong solution at that stage does harm rather than good. Nutrient strength is increased as the plant develops real leaves and starts drawing on the solution properly.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Why did only some of my sites germinate?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Usually uneven moisture rather than bad seed. A grow sponge that is dry in its middle, or one seated slightly high so it does not wick, leaves that seed without water while its neighbours are fine. Soak the medium through, squeeze out the excess, and seat each one flush.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Does the number of holes in the lid matter?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Yes, and it is a decision rather than a detail. More sites means more plants but less space each; crowded leafy greens shade one another and trap humidity, which invites disease. Fewer, wider-spaced sites suit anything that fills out. Choose the lid for the crop you intend to grow.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Where is the class held now?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>At MEOD Farm, 13 Neo Tiew Harvest Lane, Singapore 719838. The 2016 class was at Toh Orchid Farm; the venue has changed over the years, but teaching on a working farm has not.</p></div> </details> </div> <h2>Where to go next</h2> <p>Germination is the least glamorous part of hydroponics and the part that most decides whether you get a harvest. Get the first fortnight right and the rest is maintenance.</p> <ol> <li>Soak the medium properly, seat it flush, and give seedlings light early.</li> <li>Join the next <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-basic-urban-farming-with-hydroponics.html"><strong>WSQ - Basic Urban Farming with Hydroponics</strong></a> class &mdash; from <strong>$136.50 nett</strong> for eligible Singapore Citizens aged 40 and above.</li> <li>Write down what you did and what day things happened. The log is what turns one crop into a skill.</li> </ol> <p>More from this course: <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/blog/hydroponics-class-2015-geoffrey-chow-toh-orchid-farm">our 2015 class at Toh Orchid Farm</a>, <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/blog/hydroponics-first-class-2014-dr-mallick">our very first class in 2014</a>, <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/blog/urban-farming-with-hydroponics-guide">how hydroponics works</a>, <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/blog/hydroponics-kit-practical-build-singapore-december-2025">building a deep water culture kit</a>, and <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/blog/hydroponics-commercial-farm-visit-singapore-february-2020">inside a working hydroponic farm</a>.</p>', 'Tertiary Infotech Academy', 1, '2016-10-08', 'TGS-2025053916', 'TGS-2025053916', 'Hydroponic Germination Step by Step | WSQ Course Singapore', 'Most hydroponic failures happen at germination, in the first fortnight. What our 8 October 2016 class at Toh Orchid Farm covered with Geoffrey Chow: what normal progress looks like, seedling nutrient strength and spacing. WSQ funded from $136.50 nett.', 'hydroponic germination, seedling hydroponics, geoffrey chow hydroponics, toh orchid farm, hydroponics class singapore 2016, grow sponge, net pot spacing, hydroponics course singapore, wsq urban farming, skillsfuture hydroponics', 'manual-skip', NOW(), NOW() FROM DUAL WHERE @is_sg > 0 AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM (SELECT `post_id` FROM `mmd_blog_post` WHERE `url_key` = 'hydroponics-class-2016-germination-toh-orchid-farm') x);

